// Solution — Module 03, Exercise 04: Memory Leaks (Ancient Weeds)
//
// Task 1: the file as shipped ends with
//
//   spooked: ancient weeds - 1 pine never scorched (8 bytes),
//            garrisoned at line 15
//
// p1 is freed, p2 is not, so exactly one block is still standing when the
// program ends.
//
// Task 2: the warning names the line that ALLOCATED the block, not the line
// where the program ended. That is the only line that can help you — a leak
// has no site of failure, which is what makes it hard. Nothing goes wrong at
// any particular moment; the program simply never gets around to cleaning up.
//
// Task 3: scorch p2 as well and the warning disappears entirely. There is no
// "leaks: 0" line — a clean run is silent. This file is the clean version, so
// running it prints the values and nothing else.

p1 = garrison(4)
p1.poke(i32, 42)
print(f"p1 holds: {p1.peek(i32)}")

p2 = garrison(8)
p2.poke(i64, i64(999))
print(f"p2 holds: {p2.peek(i64)}")

scorch(p1)
print("p1 scorched")

// Task 3: the line the exercise leaves out. Comment it back out and the run
// ends with the ancient-weeds warning naming line 25.
scorch(p2)
print("p2 scorched")

print("")
print("both blocks freed — the run ends silent, no ancient weeds")

// Leaks report at program exit, not at the moment of the mistake, so a leak
// warning is the last thing you see rather than the first. In C nothing
// reports them at all: the process exits, the OS reclaims the pages, and the
// only symptom is a long-running program that slowly grows.
